 By default, each node is labeled with name and type, and stylized (shape, color, outline, etc.). By passing a function to the parameter ``custom_style_function``, you can customize the node style based on its attributes. This pairs nicely with the ``@tag`` function modifier (learn more :ref:`tag-decorators`)
 
+.. _display-name-tag:
+
+Set a human-readable node label
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+By default, a node is labeled with its Python function name. If you want a human-readable label in the rendered graph without renaming the function, add the ``display_name`` tag with the ``@tag`` function modifier:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    from hamilton.function_modifiers import tag
+
+    @tag(display_name="Total Revenue")
+    def total_revenue(...) -> float:
+        ...
+
+The label resolution order is: an explicitly supplied ``name`` parameter (used internally, e.g. for materializers) takes precedence, then the ``display_name`` tag value, then the node's Python identifier. This applies to node labels and to the rows of the input nodes table. If ``display_name`` is a list, its first element is used (an empty list falls back to the node name). Display names are HTML-escaped before being embedded in the label, so special characters such as ``<``, ``>``, ``&``, and quotes render correctly.
+
 Your own custom style function must:
 
 1. Use only keyword arguments, taking in ``node`` and ``node_class``.
